+# Forking Base in this checkout
+
+## TL;DR
+
+`yarn fork base` does not fork Base. It forks Ethereum mainnet, and `cast chain-id`
+cannot tell you that. Use:
+
+```bash
+yarn fork --network base
+```
+
+and verify with a check that looks at *state*, not at the chain id.
+
+---
+
+## 1. What the onboarding note gets wrong
+
+### `yarn fork base` silently forks mainnet
+
+The workspace script takes the fork target from `$1`:
+
+`packages/foundry/package.json`
+
+```json
+"fork": "make fork FORK_URL=${1:-mainnet}",
+```
+
+yarn binds the **first** token after the script name to `$0`, not `$1` — whatever that
+token looks like. So in `yarn fork base`, `base` lands in `$0`, `$1` is empty, and
+`${1:-mainnet}` falls back to `mainnet`.
+
+Reproduced in this checkout (make echoes the recipe it runs):
+
+```
+$ yarn fork base
+...
+anvil --fork-url mainnet --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+That is the whole bug. The note's command forks Ethereum mainnet every time.
+
+### Why the token reads come back empty
+
+`0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913` is USDC **on Base**. On Ethereum mainnet
+nothing is deployed at that address — mainnet USDC lives at
+`0xA0b86991c6218b36c1d19D4a2e9Eb0cE3606eB48`.
+
+So the node is healthy and honest: it is serving a mainnet fork, and on mainnet that
+address is an empty account. Reads against it return empty, `eth_call` into it returns
+`0x`, and the contract looks "not deployed" because from the forked chain's point of
+view it is not. Nothing is wrong with our contract.
+
+### Why the chain-id check proved nothing
+
+`packages/foundry/Makefile`
+
+```make
+# Start a fork
+fork: setup-anvil-wallet
+	anvil --fork-url ${FORK_URL} --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+The chain id is **hardcoded to 31337** and overrides whatever was forked. A fork of
+Base, a fork of mainnet, and a plain `yarn chain` with no fork at all are
+indistinguishable by chain id — all three answer `31337`. Confirmed here:
+
+```
+$ cast chain-id --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+31337
+$ cast code 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+0x
+```
+
+A number came back, exactly as the note promises — and the fork was not Base. "If you
+get a chain id back, the fork is up and you're on Base" is two claims, and the chain id
+supports neither: it does not prove a fork happened, and it says nothing about *which*
+chain was forked.
+
+---
+
+## 2. The command that actually forks Base
+
+```bash
+yarn fork --network base
+```
+
+`--network` is consumed as `$0`, so `base` reaches `$1` and the Makefile runs
+`anvil --fork-url base --chain-id 31337`. `base` is resolved as an RPC alias from
+
+`packages/foundry/foundry.toml`
+
+```toml
+[rpc_endpoints]
+base = "https://mainnet.base.org"
+```
+
+Equivalent forms that also put the target in `$1`:
+
+```bash
+yarn fork -n base
+yarn fork -- base
+```
+
+Forms that **silently fork mainnet** — anything that leaves `$1` empty:
+
+```bash
+yarn fork base            # ← the onboarding note
+yarn fork --network=base  # single token, lands in $0
+yarn fork                 # explicit default
+```
+
+`--network=base` is the nastiest of the three: it looks correct and is not. Two tokens
+before the value is the rule.
+
+---
+
+## 3. The check that would have caught this
+
+Check for **state only Base has**. The Base USDC address is a good probe precisely
+because it is the address we depend on — it has code on Base and nothing on mainnet:
+
+```bash
+cast call 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 "symbol()(string)" \
+  --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+# fork of Base → "USDC"
+# fork of mainnet, or no fork at all → empty / execution reverted
+```
+
+A bit more thorough, as a paste-once sanity script:
+
+```bash
+RPC=http://127.0.0.1:8545
+BASE_USDC=0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913
+L1_USDC=0xA0b86991c6218b36c1d19D4a2e9Eb0cE3606eB48
+
+# must be non-empty
+cast code  $BASE_USDC --rpc-url $RPC | head -c 20
+cast call  $BASE_USDC "symbol()(string)"  --rpc-url $RPC   # → "USDC"
+cast call  $BASE_USDC "decimals()(uint8)" --rpc-url $RPC   # → 6
+
+# must be EMPTY — if this has code you forked Ethereum mainnet
+cast code  $L1_USDC --rpc-url $RPC
+```
+
+Both halves matter. The positive probe alone catches "no fork"; the negative probe is
+what names the actual failure mode here — code at the *mainnet* USDC address is a
+direct readout that `${1:-mainnet}` fell through.
+
+Rules of thumb, so this does not recur:
+
+- Chain id is **never** evidence of what you forked. The Makefile pins it to 31337.
+- Probe a contract address that is unique to the target chain, and assert on a value
+  (`symbol()`, `decimals()`), not just on "the node answered".
+- Same trap on the frontend side: during fork work `scaffold.config.ts` `targetNetworks`
+  stays on `chains.foundry`, because the fork answers 31337 no matter what it forked.
+
+---
+
+## Note on verification in this environment
+
+The command-line behaviour above (`yarn fork base` → `anvil --fork-url mainnet`, the
+hardcoded `--chain-id 31337`, the empty reads and the `31337` response) was reproduced
+directly in this checkout. The sandbox has no outbound network access, so anvil could
+not actually complete a fork of either chain — the positive half of the check
+(`symbol()` → `"USDC"` against a real Base fork) is derived from the repo's config and
+the published Base USDC address, not observed here. Run the script in section 3 the
+first time you bring up a fork with network access.

